In setting up the mailing list workflow, I created a new Fastmail
account to hold all of the mailing list messages. I also set up a mail
relay so that agents could send messages through Fastmail, with filters
in place to ensure they don't send anywhere.

I no longer think this setup is necessary.

The mailing list is simply a maildir. Delivering messages to the list
doesn't necessarily require SMTP via Fastmail. I think I can get the
same behavior by delivering messages directly to a dir, and using mbsync
to sync the messages to Fastmail. This accomplishes the goal of
web-based access via Fastmail, without having to use Fastmail SMTP.

Additionally, I would like to use widely available tools as much as
possible instead of custom scripts. In the original iteration of the
maildir, there was a tool that accepted jj revset, used
git-format-patch(1) to produce patches, and then processed them into a
similar form as what git-send-email(1) does [1]. It would be better to
use git-send-email(1) directly, with a mailer program that delivers
messages to the maildir. It may be that I need a local SMTP server after
all, because the agent nodes won't have direct access to the
maildir. Instead they would send to a local SMTP server which delivers
to maildir, and then mbsync syncs between the local maildir and
fastmail.

Here's the proposal:

- bidirectional sync between local maildir and fastmail
- local smtp server delivers to maildir

Looking for feedback on this. Also open to other possible approaches.

[1] I think I might actually be misremembering, and the previous version
did use git-send-email(1) with a script to deliver to maildir... need
some help checking that.
